abstract: >-
  FluBNF forecasts weekly influenza hospital admissions for the 52 US
  jurisdictions of the CDC FluSight challenge. It submits two standalone
  models, each under its own hub identity, and blends nothing. The Oracle
  SIHRS fits an SIHRS compartment model each week with the Liu-West filter:
  in public PyBNF (lanl/PyBNF at 8410f794, job type lwf) from the 2026-11-14
  round, and in the lab's private PyBNF fork on bngsim before it. Each
  forecast path's growth is then combined, by a weighted geometric mean,
  with donor growth from earlier seasons at the same calendar week, at
  weight one quarter on the filter's growth from the 2026-10-17 round (one
  half before).
  Groundhog is empirical: the last observed count scaled by the empirical
  quantiles of growth ratios seen at the same epiweek in strictly earlier
  seasons, pooled across jurisdictions with the jurisdiction's own past
  weeks carrying half of the weight and donor weeks whose count is near
  the jurisdiction's newest weighted most (size-matched donors), with a
  committed FluSurv-NET donor bank spliced in; nothing is fitted. In
  vintage-true retrospective replays of the 2023-24, 2024-25 and 2025-26
  seasons, scored as weighted interval score relative to the CDC
  FluSight-baseline (ratio of WIS sums on shared cells), the Groundhog's
  shipped form, own-state weighting with size-matched donors, pools at
  0.642 (0.680, 0.627 and 0.645 by season) on 17,116 cells under
  FluSight's cell rule. In vintage-true retrospective replays of 2023-24
  to 2025-26 under its registered form (weight one half, the season-to-date
  seed, no narrowing or cap, on the private fork), the Oracle SIHRS pools
  at 0.738 against the plain Liu-West filter's 0.819 on 15,300 cells under
  the earlier cell rule (truth and median above 0). The settings submitted
  since October have not been scored on those cells. All
  scores are self-computed from the hub's archived vintage data and
  baseline files, and are retrospective replays rather than real-time
  submissions. The 1.0 and 1.1 releases shipped a
  different product, an equal-weight ensemble; their record is in
  docs/archive/RELEASE-1.0.md.
